Z||000000||varnster||08-07-2001||09:15 PM||||so hot! no knuckles left! tools all over the<BR>yard, on top of the house.cant get alternator<BR>out of my 87gt. the Hanes manual is no friging help. it is all unbolted but wont come out,I have tried the archives but no luck, HAZE what did you finally do?||||reg||8
Z||000001||AL||08-07-2001||09:27 PM||||take the fan of the alternator off,then slide it out from the bottom,also take the wheel off first,that's how i did it.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <BR>84 Indy 4 speed (stole it)250$<BR>86GT rebuilt<BR>89 ws6 GTA <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||08-07-2001||09:32 PM||||LOL.  I can relate.  I got pissed off at mine and took out the rear cradle bolts and used a floor jack to lower the rear of the cradle enough to get the alternator along with the POS broken bracket out of the car.  A belt change turned in to a whole day job.<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||08-07-2001||10:01 PM||||Or you can pop the outer control rod out and move it out of the way and let it fall out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||08-07-2001||10:40 PM||||I have done one on my v6 stick and two on the auto and always brought them up through the top with no problem. Just removed the cooling duct for the fan and the alt bracket. Takes me about 30 min to r/r and put in a new voltage regulator.||||reg||1
Z||000005||varnster||08-07-2001||10:54 PM||||to take off the alt bracket means disconecting the dogbone, this bracket is <BR>what i have used in the past to support the <BR>motor {pulling the oil pan) so where is a <BR>good spot to use a jack to support the motor?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero*101||08-07-2001||10:58 PM||||Hey guys <BR>i know exactly what you guys are going thru<P>i just got mine in yesterday<P>i broke my alt bracket then i thought i could weld it but it was to small and since its alum... its very weak so dont put to much pressure on it.<P>Fiero*101||||reg||1
Z||000007||PBJ||08-07-2001||11:01 PM||||you don't have to support the mtr when you disconnect the dog bone.  It is only a torque mount, not a support mount.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bluebottlejunky||08-07-2001||11:17 PM||||"I" found best way with the 85 gt 4sp..is to go under the car..worked great.!<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||08-08-2001||07:57 AM||||On my 85GT auto I took the cooling tubes off the firewall and the dog bone strut bracket off and pulled the alternator out the top.||||reg||1
Z||000010||varnster||08-08-2001||08:49 AM||||I think I will try pulling it out the top, <BR>the only way to go out the bottom is to disconect the exhaust, or the control arms,<BR>or sawing the d--n thing in half. sorry you<BR>guys that saw it just falls out the bottom are nuts, at least on this model fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000011||JohnF||08-08-2001||09:06 AM||||Just had a new GM Delco put in yesterday.  No problem - just a couple of hundred buck!||||reg||12
Z||000012||JohnA||08-08-2001||09:22 AM||||I have a 1988 Formula, the altenator went and I took one look at it and had a garage do it. They said $250 and when I got there it was $350. Told me only one unit fit the car and it was expensive because it was a specially made unit??? oh well, it's done and it drives great. At least it will charge for awhile. <BR>My understanding from a factory manual I have, was that you had to remove the wheel and the inner fender to get at it and hope the bolts loosened.<BR>JOHN||||reg||4
Z||000013||LAMBO||08-08-2001||10:11 AM||||Out the top. Helps to remove cooling tube first. Just take a couple deep breaths and everything will be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000014||bHooper||08-08-2001||11:08 AM||||maybe i missed it, but is it an auto or manual?<P>if it's a manual, remove the altenator heatshield, adn the thing will just about drop straight down!  <P>if it's an auto... well, you may want to see the sectiona bout tilting the cradle to fix exhaust manifolds, and change the spark plugs.  cause i could begin to tell you what to do there.  heck, it took me 3 months and Rosie Parks chuckleing at my chirping belt to finally get the  motivation to tighten the altenator and a/c belts on my v-6 automatic!<P>gest of luck<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||08-08-2001||12:41 PM||||im an expert as ive done it several times already on mine. I just raise the back of car, loosen belt tension bolt. take out the alum cooling tube (couple of 10mm nuts) disconnect battery, then remove leads to alternator from underneath. then take out pivot bolt, and drop it out the bottom. Whole thing takes me about 15-20 mins. dont have to touch exhaust or wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000016||StuGood||08-08-2001||12:48 PM||||Never had the pleasure... never an alternator failure yet!  Of course, now that I bragged on them, they'll all fail witin a month  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>Well, good luck, varnster, and when you get ready to throw another tool on the roof, just be glad it's an <B>alternator</B> and not a generator (15+ pounds of cast iron, etc,)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   ||||reg||12
Z||000017||varnster||08-08-2001||12:53 PM||||This is an automatic and the passenger exhaust pipe is uder the alternator its<BR>a half day job taking the exhaust off and<BR>rehanging it. there is no way it comes out of the bottom on automatic, maybe out the wheel well if you completly remove the alt bracket and possibly the control arm.||||reg||1
Z||000018||pherder||08-08-2001||01:14 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by varnster:<BR><B>This is an automatic and the passenger exhaust pipe is under the alternator its<BR>a half day job taking the exhaust off and<BR>rehanging it. there is no way it comes out of the bottom on automatic, maybe out the wheel well if you completely remove the alt bracket and possibly the control arm.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I <B>JUST</B> got done replacing my alternator in my 86 SE V6 auto car.<P>There is <B>NO</B> way it will fit thru the top.  I took off all that was mentioned above and still there was no way to remove it.  Seems the bracket for the heat shield, and the brake line are in the way along with the exhaust manifold. <P>I removed the tie rod from the knuckle and moved it as far left as it would go, removed the alternator bracket and <B>THEN</B> the alternator would go thru the wheelwell (after removing the back of the splash guard and the wheel, of course.)<P>What a <B>PAIN!!!!</B>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Did I mention it is 93 degrees out (F) and relative humidity is somewhere above the rainbow (although what is RAIN???)<P>Next time I will just drop the engine out, it might be quicker and a hell of a lot easier!!!<P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 08-08-2001).]||||reg||8
